  "synonym_discrimination": {
    "words": [
      "thing-n",
      "object-n",
      "item-n",
      "stuff-n"
    ],
    "note": "**Thing** is the general word, used when you do not know or do not need the exact name: *What's that thing?* An **object** is a solid thing that can be seen and touched, and the word is more formal: *a small metal object*. An **item** is one thing in a list, a shop, or a collection: *the last item on the list*. **Stuff** is informal and uncountable, for things together without saying what they are: *Put your stuff in the car*."
  },
  "usage_note": "**Thing** is vague on purpose. Speakers use it when the exact word is unknown, unimportant, or would sound too formal, and in speech it sounds natural. In careful writing it is better to name the thing when you can: **object**, **matter**, **factor**, **feature**, **event**. **Things** in the plural often means the situation in general: *How are things?*",
  "learner_errors": [],
  "etymology": null,
  "see_also": [
    {
      "slug": "stuff-n",
      "note": null
    }
  ],
  "adaptation": {
    "semantic": "**Thing** can stand for an object, an action, an event, a matter, an idea, or even a person, so other languages often need several words for it; choose by what **thing** replaces in the sentence. **Things** in the plural often means the situation in general and may translate as **everything** or **life**.",
    "grammar": "Countable and always with a determiner or in the plural: *a thing*, *the thing*, *things*. Where some languages use a mass word, English often uses the plural **things** or the uncountable **stuff**. After negatives, **a thing** means anything at all.",
    "culture": null,
    "false_friends": null,
    "pronunciation": "**Th** is the sound of **think** and **thin**, not of **this**, made with the tongue between the teeth; the word ends in the **ng** sound of **sing** with no **g** sound after it. **Things** ends with a **z** sound."
  },
